INT. STALLED CABLE CAR - GORGE CROSSING - DAY

A steel box the size of a closet, hanging from one cable over a drop of four hundred meters. Wind pushes at the glass. The motor has been dead for six hours.

IMANI OKORO (30s, park ranger, sunburnt neck, frayed jacket) sits on the floor with her back against the door. PETR HALAS (60s, retired surveyor, wool cap, thermos) stands, one hand on the rail.

The car sways. They both freeze. It settles. They both breathe out.

IMANI
Thermos. How much is left?

PETR
Half a cup. Maybe less.

IMANI
We split it at dusk.

PETR
We split it now. Dusk is a rumor.

IMANI
Petr.

PETR
Fine. Dusk.

Imani pulls the radio from her belt, thumbs the switch. Static. She tries again. Static. She throws it onto the bench, then fetches it back.

IMANI
(to herself)
Batteries are fine. Battery's fine. It's the gorge.

PETR
Say again?

IMANI
Nothing. The walls eat the signal.

PETR
Then we stop wasting the battery on it.

IMANI
We have the battery for exactly this.

PETR
We have the battery for three more tries. I counted your tries.

IMANI
You counted.

PETR
I measured land for forty years. I count things.

IMANI
Then count this. Rescue knows the line stopped at nine this morning. They know there are two cars on the north span. They send a crew when the wind drops under forty.

PETR
And when does the wind drop under forty?

IMANI
Tonight. Tomorrow at the latest.

PETR
You said that at noon.

IMANI
At noon it was true.

PETR
At noon you also said the cable would hold the whole day, no problem.

IMANI
The cable is rated for six times our weight.

PETR
Rated by whom?

IMANI
By people who do not want to be in here with you.

PETR
That is almost funny.

IMANI
I am almost laughing.

PETR
My knees are going. I would like to sit.

IMANI
Then sit. There is room.

PETR
There is room if you move your boots.

IMANI
My boots are not the problem.

PETR
Your boots are the whole problem.

Imani draws her legs in. Petr lowers himself onto the bench opposite and winces. The car rocks. They grab for each other's sleeves, then pull back, embarrassed.

PETR (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Forty years on solid ground.

Imani unzips her jacket and spreads it over his knees without a word.

PETR (CONT'D)
I am not cold.

IMANI
I know.

The wind drops a notch. Far below, a river keeps talking to itself.

IMANI (CONT'D)
Hear that? Wind's easing.

PETR
Or I am going deaf.

IMANI
Either way, we are closer to morning.

PETR
Pour the coffee, ranger.

She does. Half a cup, divided with great care.
